  "content": {
    "id": "p66-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "",
    "prompt": "Find the values for $k$ for which $x^{2}+(k+1)x+4=0$ has real roots.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Real roots require $\\Delta\\geq0$: $k\\leq-5$ or $k\\geq3$.",
      "worked": "Real roots require $\\Delta\\geq0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=(k+1)^{2}-4(1)(4)\\\\&=k^{2}+2k-15\\\\k^{2}+2k-15&\\geq0\\\\(k+5)(k-3)&\\geq0\\end{align*}$$\nThe parabola $y=k^{2}+2k-15$ opens upwards with intercepts $-5$ and $3$. It is on or above the axis outside these intercepts.\n$$k\\leq-5\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\geq3$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 45
  },
